\section{Recognized input columns}{

Detection is case-insensitive, as in \code{\link[=predict_names]{predict_names()}}: any subset of
the name fields \code{first}, \code{middle}, \code{last}, \code{maiden} and the
geography fields \code{zcta}, \code{tract}, \code{block_group}, \code{block}. When
\code{geo_dict} is supplied, a \code{geoid} column is also recognized and takes
precedence; its values (and, with \code{geo_dict}, the values of any other
geography column) are matched against \code{geo_dict$geoid} as-is, with no
normalization. With the bundled geography tables the usual ID
normalization applies (ZIP zero-padding, \code{"1400000US"} /
\code{"1500000US"} / \code{"7500000US"} prefix stripping) and the most specific
column wins (\code{block} > \code{block_group} > \code{tract} > \code{zcta}).
}

\section{Block-level geography and the block-group fallback}{

A \code{block} column holds 15-digit 2020 Census Block GEOIDs and is
matched against \link{geo_block_vap}, which covers the 5,704,969 blocks
with any voting-age population (50 states, DC, and Puerto Rico) and
stores integer VAP counts (row-normalized to proportions before the
fold). Two situations reroute a row to the block's parent block
group (\code{substr(geoid, 1, 12)}), each reported once per call via
\code{\link[=message]{message()}} (suppressible with \code{\link[=suppressMessages]{suppressMessages()}}):
\itemize{
\item \code{geography_type = "cvap"} — no block-level CVAP table exists
(citizenship is not collected in the decennial census), so every
block row uses its block group's CVAP proportions instead.
\item A block GEOID not among the populated 2020 blocks (zero-VAP
block, or an ID that does not exist). With
\code{block_fallback = TRUE} (default) such rows use their block
group's VAP proportions, and the message reports how many rows
fell back; with \code{block_fallback = FALSE} they get no geography
component (name-only posterior), and the message reports how
many rows were skipped.
}
Populated-block rows additionally blend \code{block_shrink} pseudo-people
drawn from the parent block group's composition into the block's
counts before normalizing (default \code{10}; \code{0} restores the raw
counts) — see \code{\link[=geo_prior]{geo_prior()}}, \strong{Block-count shrinkage toward the
block group}, for the formula and the validation behind the
default.

\code{block_fallback} and \code{block_shrink} are ignored when \code{geo_dict} is
supplied (a \code{block} column is then matched against \code{geo_dict$geoid}
as-is, like any other geography column).
}

\section{User-supplied name dictionaries}{

\code{name_dict} may be a single data frame (used for given names and
surnames alike) or a named list with elements \code{first} and/or \code{last}.
Each dictionary needs a \code{name} column plus two or more numeric
category columns; a \code{frequency} column is optional and, when
present, is used to derive the marginal prior. Rows are renormalized
to sum to 1; names are normalized like \code{\link[=normalize_name]{normalize_name()}} before
indexing. Lookup uses the same five-step cascade as \code{\link[=lookup_name]{lookup_name()}},
with the same cross-dictionary fallback as the bundled path: given
names try \code{first} then \code{last}, surnames try \code{last} then \code{first}
(whichever of the two are available). When both \code{first} and \code{last}
are supplied their category columns must match.

Because the categories are arbitrary, the bundled sex table works as
a dictionary too:
\code{predict_demog(df, name_dict = list(first = first_names_sex))}
returns \code{p_male} / \code{p_female}.
}

\section{User-supplied geography}{

\code{geo_dict} is a data frame with a \code{geoid} column plus one numeric
column per category (a \code{total} column is optional and used only for
prior derivation). Rows are renormalized to sum to 1. When both
\code{name_dict} and \code{geo_dict} are supplied, their category columns must
agree (order may differ; the name dictionary's order wins). When
only \code{geo_dict} is supplied and name columns are present, the
bundled name tables are used, so \code{geo_dict} must use the six
\code{\link[=race_groups]{race_groups()}} categories.
}

\section{The marginal prior}{

The Naive-Bayes combination divides by \code{P(category)^(k-1)} and the
geography fold divides by \code{P(category)} once. The prior is taken
from, in order: the \code{prior} argument; the \code{prior} attribute of the
supplied \code{last} (then \code{first}) dictionary; a frequency-weighted
average of the supplied dictionary's rows; a \code{total}-weighted
average of \code{geo_dict}; the bundled Census prior when the categories
are the six bundled ones. If none of these applies, a uniform prior
is used and a warning is raised whenever the prior materially enters
the computation (some row has more than one evidence piece, or a
name posterior is folded with geography).
}

